      "text": "Tentative Schedule Of Topics\n\n1. Course overview; introduction to operating systems\n2. Quick review of computer architecture; Process management: process scheduling and context switching\n3. Process management: process suspension and resumption; message passing\n4. Process management: process coordination and synchronization\n5. Low-level memory management: address spaces, stack and heap segments\n6. High-level memory management: partitioning and virtual memory; demand paging\n7. High-level synchronous message passing\n8. Device management: exceptions, interrupts, and device drivers\n9. Device management: device-independent I/O and an example device driver\n10. Networking and protocol implementation; network processes\n11. Remote disk and remote file access mechanisms\n12. File systems, file names, and a syntactic namespace\n13. Pipes and user interface (shell)\n14. Operating system configuration and initialization\n15. Multicore operating systems\n\nCourse overview; introduction to operating systems\nQuick review of computer architecture; Process management: process scheduling and context switching\n\nProcess management: process suspension and resumption; message passing\nProcess management: process coordination and synchronization\n\nLow-level memory management: address spaces, stack and heap segments\nHigh-level memory management: partitioning and virtual memory; demand paging\nHigh-level synchronous message passing\n\nMulticore operating systems\n\nNote: the selection of topics and the order may change to accommodate lab assignment not yet finalized due to the set of lab assignments).\n\nAttendance\n\nStudents are expected to be present for every meeting of the classes in which they are enrolled. Only the student can excuse a student from a course requirement or responsibility. When confederates or absences can be repaired such as for many University sponsored activities and religious observances, the student should monitor the structure of the situation as far as advance as possible and plain to make up for missed work.\n\nChanges For Emergencies\n\nIn the event of a major campus emergency, course requirements, deadlines and grading percentages are subject to change that may be necessitated by a revised semester calendar or other circumstances. If an emergency is found to be necessary, the CS web page for details.\n\nSimiles with Disabilities\n\nIn order to improve the quality of the models of students with disabilities as outlined in both the\n(1) and (2) of the Student and Student Disabilities Act of 1990 through the provision of anన్‌fayards\n(3) and (4) of the Student and Student Disabilities Act of 1990 through the provision of anన్‌fayards\n(5) and (6) of the Student and Student Disabilities Act of 1990 through the provision of anన్‌fayards\n(7) and (8) of the Student and Student Disabilities Act of 1990 through the provision of anన్‌fayards\n(9) and (10) of the Student and Student Disabilities Act of 1990 through the provision of anన్‌fayards"
